        "id": "working-across-fields",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Where disciplines meet",
        "content": "Some research questions sit between familiar university departments. Understanding perception can require experiments with human participants alongside computational models; managing a protected landscape can require ecological evidence alongside knowledge of livelihoods. Iranian examples illustrate both approaches, although the available institutional records describe particular activities rather than the scale of interdisciplinary research across the country. [s1][s3]\n\nA useful way to read these examples is to ask what each field contributes. Interdisciplinarity becomes tangible when researchers combine methods, data, or interpretations around a shared question. Simply bringing institutions together establishes an opportunity for collaboration; evidence of completed research requires a further step. This distinction matters especially when reading announcements of new agreements. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "cognition-and-computation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Cognition, experiments, and computing",
        "content": "A research recruitment page from Mehdi Sanayei’s laboratory describes projects associated with the School of Cognitive Sciences at the Institute for Research in Fundamental Sciences, commonly abbreviated IPM. Its doctoral information identifies entry routes through cognitive sciences, electrical engineering, and computer engineering. The laboratory’s stated work includes psychophysics, cognitive modelling, and neural data analysis. [s1]\n\nPsychophysics investigates relationships between physical stimuli and perception. The advertised human-participant projects concern time perception and predictive processing, while separate analysis projects use existing animal datasets to examine the thalamus in behaviour and cognition. Programming in Python or MATLAB appears alongside neuroscience knowledge among the requested skills. These are concrete examples of experimental and computational expertise meeting within a research programme. The recruitment page does not establish clinical effectiveness, national research capacity, or whether its advertised positions remain open. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "environment-and-cultural-history",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Environmental questions and cultural knowledge",
        "content": "A September 2026 announcement from the UNESCO Chair on Coastal Geo-Hazards Analysis describes a memorandum linking the chair, the Research Institute for Earth Sciences at the Geological Survey of Iran, and the Natural Disasters Research Institute with its UNESCO Chair on Natural Disasters Management. Its proposed subjects extend from drought, climate, and remote sensing to geo-archaeology, past climates, and historical or literary studies concerning Persian myths. [s2]\n\nThe agreement provides for joint projects, publications, researcher exchanges, and meetings to identify priorities. Financing is to be negotiated, and particular activities may require separate agreements. It therefore documents an agreed framework and research intentions, rather than completed discoveries. [s2]\n\nA separate UNESCO project record links evidence about natural resources and biodiversity with sustainable livelihoods at designated sites, including biosphere reserves, geoparks, and World Heritage sites. It also identifies green-economy skills and site-management capacity as intended contributions. Read together, these records show how environmental work can connect scientific evidence with management and social questions. They do not demonstrate that the intended environmental or livelihood improvements have already occurred. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "collaboration-framework",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "What the coastal geo-hazards memorandum establishes",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  A[\"Coastal Geo-Hazards Chair\"] -->|\"Participates in\"| D[\"Research cooperation framework\"]\n  B[\"Research Institute for Earth Sciences\"] -->|\"Participates in\"| D\n  C[\"Natural Disasters Institute and Chair\"] -->|\"Participates in\"| D\n  D -->|\"Provides for\"| E[\"Joint research and publications\"]\n  D -->|\"Provides for\"| F[\"Researcher exchanges and meetings\"]\n  D -->|\"Leaves to further negotiation\"| G[\"Activity financing and implementation details\"]\n```",
        "summary": "The arrows identify participation and provisions of the announced agreement. They do not represent supervisory authority, confirmed funding, or completed research outputs.",
        "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "assessing-achievement",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Recognizing an achievement",
        "content": "These examples offer different kinds of evidence: a laboratory describes research methods and training expectations; a memorandum sets out cooperation; a project portal records intended management activities. Each helps explain how fields connect, but none alone measures national success. Assessing achievement requires matching the claim to the evidence—for example, examining published results for a scientific finding or an evaluation for an asserted practical benefit. [s1][s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      }
