    "seo": {
      "title": "Education Challenges in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand documented pressures on education access, learning, digital participation, skills development, and continuity of schooling in Iran."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "understanding-the-challenges",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Access, learning, and continuity",
        "content": "Evaluating education requires several distinct questions. Can learners enter and remain in education? Do they acquire the intended knowledge? Can they continue learning when buildings or services are disrupted? UNESCO’s education monitoring separates participation, completion, learning proficiency, resources, and teacher indicators. That structure helps explain why a single enrolment statistic cannot describe the whole system. [s1]\n\nFor Iran, the inspected evidence supports a connected account of these pressures, but with uneven coverage. UNHCR documents school-capacity constraints affecting displaced learners, while reporting on remote education identifies equipment and connectivity barriers for vulnerable Iranian and refugee households. More recent humanitarian reporting records physical disruption to education. These sources concern different populations and reporting periods; their findings should not be combined into an undated national failure rate. [s2][s3][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "finance-and-capacity",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Resources and classroom capacity",
        "content": "The annual series below reports education expenditure relative to gross domestic product, the value of economic output. This ratio provides context for the resources allocated to education, but does not show spending per learner, purchasing power, the distribution of funds, or classroom effectiveness. Its missing observations must remain visible. [s1]\n\nCapacity pressures are more tangible in UNHCR’s reporting. Its update covering April 2025 describes overcrowded classrooms in refugee-hosting areas and attributes an estimate of additional national school-construction needs to the government. These are reported needs, rather than an independently completed census of school conditions. They nevertheless show why an entitlement to education can coexist with difficulty obtaining an available place. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "education-finance-trend",
        "type": "chart",
        "content": {
          "type": "line",
          "title": "Iran: government education expenditure as a share of GDP",
          "dataset_id": "education-expenditure-gdp",
          "x_field": "period_label",
          "series_ids": ["education-gdp-share"],
          "x_axis_label": "Source reporting year",
          "y_axis_label": "Percent of GDP, 0–100 scale",
          "y_axis_min": 0,
          "show_legend": false,
          "connect_nulls": false,
          "interpretation": "The inspected UIS profile reports a higher share in 2018 than in 2013, followed by lower reported shares in 2022 and 2023. The gap in 2021 prevents an uninterrupted annual reading. These ratios do not establish changes in real spending per student."
        },
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "learning-and-digital-access",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Participation does not establish learning",
        "content": "UNESCO reports minimum proficiency separately from participation and completion. This is a useful distinction for reading claims about education quality: attendance records show involvement in schooling, while assessments investigate particular learning outcomes. Neither should be treated as a complete measure of a child’s educational experience. The inspected country profile also contains substantial gaps, limiting what can be said about recent change. [s1]\n\nDuring pandemic closures, UNHCR reported that students could in principle use Shaad, Iran’s remote-learning application, but that practical access depended on devices and connectivity. Its account explicitly identified difficulties for vulnerable refugees and Iranians. This historical evidence explains a mechanism of unequal participation: providing a platform does not establish that every household can use it. It does not measure the extent of digital exclusion in 2026. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "education-to-work",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "From education to working life",
        "content": "Skills and employment form another part of the challenge. UNICEF’s announcement of the TORANJ programme describes an initiative joining education, life readiness, and employment-related skills in Kermanshah, Isfahan, Shahrood, Qom, and Qeshm. Its emphasis on local capacities indicates that interventions need to respond to particular settings. [s5]\n\nA launch announcement establishes the programme’s stated purpose and reported operation, rather than its lasting effect on employment. Participant testimonials are also different from a representative evaluation. This distinction applies more broadly to claims that a training course, university partnership, or entrepreneurship scheme has resolved a skills mismatch. Such claims require evidence about subsequent outcomes and the people included in the assessment. [s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s5"]
      },
      {
        "id": "emergency-continuity",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Keeping learning possible during emergencies",
        "content": "Emergency preparedness was already an education concern before the 2026 hostilities. UNICEF and the Iranian Red Crescent Society reported training volunteers in inclusive early childhood education during emergencies, including child protection, communication, disability inclusion, and psychosocial support. The activity demonstrates preparation and training, rather than proof that every affected child received uninterrupted education. [s6]\n\nOCHA’s update covering late March and early April 2026 subsequently described school-damage assessments, closures where buildings were unsafe, and schools being used as temporary shelters. It also reported efforts to rehabilitate schools, support connectivity, and establish temporary learning provision. These conditions add safety and continuity to longer-standing access questions. The update is a dated emergency snapshot; it does not establish the condition of all schools at the October editorial cutoff. [s4]\n\nThe most useful reading of Iran’s education challenges therefore keeps resources, access, learning, and later opportunities distinct while examining their connections. It also keeps historical observations separate from current conditions. This overview remains incomplete on student migration, graduate employment, and university–industry outcomes, which require additional dedicated evidence.",
        "source_ids": ["s4", "s6"]
      }
