    "description": "Educational equity concerns whether learners can participate and learn despite differences in disability, household circumstances, location, or documentation status. Evidence from Iran shows why formal access must be assessed alongside accessible materials, practical support, and available school places.",
    "tags": ["Educational equity", "Inclusive education", "Disability", "Refugee education", "School access"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T18:00:04Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T18:00:04Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Educational Equity in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Examine documented barriers to inclusive schooling in Iran, including disability access, learning materials, geography, and refugee documentation."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "equity-in-practice",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What equitable participation requires",
        "content": "A school place is only part of educational access. Learners also need to enter the building, obtain usable materials, communicate in the classroom, and receive appropriate support. UNICEF’s account of disability inclusion in Iran identifies shortcomings in accessible infrastructure and learning resources, alongside weaknesses in identification and data collection. This makes equity a question of how education is delivered as well as who is formally admitted. [s1]\n\nDifferent barriers can overlap. A child with a disability in a remote community may face both accessibility difficulties and limited nearby provision. Research by Human Rights Watch and the Center for Human Rights in Iran identified particular exclusion risks for girls with disabilities and children living in rural or remote areas. These findings concern the research period of their report; they should not be presented as a new measurement of present-day prevalence. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "disability-and-support",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Disability: admission and meaningful inclusion",
        "content": "The organizations’ report describes barriers associated with school-entry assessments, reluctant admissions, inaccessible facilities, and inadequate classroom support. Its evidence came from interviews and documentary research, with important sampling and access limitations. It establishes documented experiences and mechanisms of exclusion, rather than a representative national rate. [s2]\n\nUNICEF describes work with Iranian partners on teacher capacity, disability identification and referral, assistive technologies, and better information systems. These activities address different parts of the pathway into learning: identifying support needs does not by itself make a classroom accessible, while equipment alone does not supply trained assistance. Programme intentions must therefore be distinguished from independently demonstrated changes in participation. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "accessible-materials",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Learning materials as essential infrastructure",
        "content": "Braille provides a concrete example of the resources required for inclusion. In February 2025, UNICEF reported procuring a braille embosser for the Ministry of Education and Special Education Organization to improve textbook production. Its account describes older machines as unreliable and attributes difficulty procuring replacement equipment directly to international sanctions. These are UNICEF’s descriptions of the intervention and its procurement context. [s3]\n\nThe distinction between delivering a machine and improving a learner’s experience remains important. The announcement establishes procurement and the intended contribution to accessible materials. It does not independently verify that every pupil subsequently received the right textbook before classes began. Evaluating equity would also require evidence on delivery, usability, and classroom support. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "documentation-and-school-places",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Afghan learners and changing administrative barriers",
        "content": "UNHCR’s factsheet covering January–September 2024 describes foreign children studying alongside Iranian pupils, but also records constraints: registration depended on a Yekta identification code and available school capacity. Its account demonstrates why broad statements about inclusive policy need to be read alongside the administrative conditions operating in a particular school year. [s4]\n\nA subsequent UNHCR update reports an April 2025 instruction in Tehran province prohibiting registration of students holding headcount slips for the following academic year. This was a specific reported restriction affecting a documentation category, rather than evidence that every Afghan child was excluded everywhere. The update does not establish whether that instruction was later modified or how consistently it was implemented. [s5]\n\nAssessing educational equity therefore requires information about both rules and lived access, disaggregated by relevant circumstances. The evidence here documents important barriers and responses, but a complete national account still requires current data on household income, geography, gender, language, disability, and documentation status.",
        "source_ids": ["s4", "s5"]
      }
    ],
